## Spatial Distribution Analysis — May 26, 2026

### Disturbed vs Relaxed Clusters in the Cosmic Web

To visualize whether disturbed and relaxed clusters occupy different
regions of large scale structure, we mapped the sky and comoving
positions of all 4,089 eROSITA supercluster member clusters with
D_COMB morphology classifications.

**Figures:**
- `supercluster_spatial_distribution.png` — Sky map, RA vs redshift,
  and comoving 3D distribution
- `supercluster_mass_distribution.png` — Sky distribution colored
  by cluster mass for disturbed (red) and relaxed (blue) separately

---

### Key Finding — Complete Spatial Mixing

Disturbed and relaxed clusters are thoroughly intermixed throughout
the cosmic web. No spatial separation is observed at any scale —
sky position, redshift, or 3D comoving coordinates.

| Population | RA range | Dec range | z range | Mean mass |
|-----------|---------|---------|---------|-----------|
| Disturbed | 0.1–359.4° | −88.0 to +40.7° | 0.013–0.985 | 23.41×10¹⁴ M☉ |
| Relaxed | 0.0–359.9° | −89.1 to +41.0° | 0.009–0.811 | 22.35×10¹⁴ M☉ |

Mass difference: t=1.65, p=0.098 — consistent with equal mass
distributions. Both populations cover identical sky area and
redshift range.

---

### Mass Distribution — Same Nodes, Different Clocks

The mass-colored sky maps reveal a striking pattern: the most massive
disturbed clusters and the most massive relaxed clusters occupy
**exactly the same cosmic web nodes** — the same high-density
supercluster environments, the same filament intersections.

This is physically meaningful. The densest nodes of the cosmic web
contain massive clusters in all stages of the merger cycle
simultaneously:
- Some currently merging — disturbed, potential shallowed
- Some recently completed mergers — re-virialized, potential
  re-deepening
- Some not yet merged — relaxed, potential stable

Environment and mass determine WHERE a cluster lives.
Dynamical state determines WHEN in the merger cycle it currently is.

Same cosmic address. Different clock reading.

---

### Connection to Parks ISW Morphology Signal

This spatial analysis directly supports the mass-parity ISW result
(Parks 2026, main paper). Equal mass clusters in the same cosmic
web environments show systematically different CMB temperatures
based purely on their dynamical state — cold for post-shock
re-virialization, warm for long-term relaxed.

The spatial plots confirm that disturbed and relaxed clusters of
equal mass ARE co-located in the same supercluster environments.
The ISW signal is therefore measuring the gravitational phase —
the clock reading — of clusters that share the same cosmic address.

**ΔT ∝ (Φ_exit − Φ_entry)**

Same environment. Same mass. Same cosmic address.
Different gravitational phase. Different CMB temperature.

That is the mass parity signal.

