### Mass-Matched Balance Test Result

When controlling for mass within each supercluster — matching
disturbed to relaxed clusters within 20% mass tolerance:

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Superclusters analyzed | 390 |
| Superclusters with mass-matched pairs | 184 |
| Total matched pairs | 236 |
| Match rate | 27.5% |
| Bootstrap significance vs random | −19.17σ (below random) |

After mass matching the balance signal disappears — in fact
equal-mass pairs are significantly LESS likely to co-occur within
the same supercluster than random expectation.

**Interpretation:** The 4.29σ balance result from the median split
reflects uniform distribution of the full morphology spectrum across
superclusters — not pairing of equal-mass disturbed and relaxed
clusters. Equal mass clusters in different dynamical states tend to
be in DIFFERENT superclusters. Same mass, different cosmic address,
different clock reading.

This does not weaken the ISW result — it refines the physical
picture. The ISW mass-parity signal measures individual cluster
gravitational phase independently of environment. The supercluster
balance measures population-level morphology uniformity across
large scale structure. These are complementary observables.

---

### Physical Summary

The cosmic web distributes cluster dynamical states uniformly:
- Every supercluster contains the full spectrum of merger stages
- Most massive clusters cluster in the same high-density nodes
  regardless of dynamical state
- Equal mass clusters in different dynamical states tend to occupy
  different supercluster environments
- The merger cycle plays out independently in each cosmic web node

The ISW morphology signal — cold for disturbed, warm for relaxed —
is a gravitational phase detector operating independently of
large scale structure environment. Two clusters of equal mass
anywhere in the universe will show different CMB temperatures
based solely on where they are in the merger cycle.

